    written: [
      { q: "Who headed the Second Backward Classes Commission?", a: "B.P. Mandal.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the BJP-led alliance that ruled the country from 1998 to 2004.", a: "The National Democratic Alliance (NDA), under A.B. Vajpayee.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is a coalition government? Give one example from the 1989–2014 period.", a: "1. A government formed by an alliance of two or more parties when no single party has a majority in the legislature.\n2. Example: the National Front (1989), the United Front (1996), the NDA (1998–2004) or the UPA (2004–14).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two reasons why the year 1989 is seen as a turning point in Indian politics.", a: "1. The Congress was defeated in the 1989 elections, marking the end of the Congress system.\n2. A period of coalition governments began, in which regional parties became important partners; Mandal and the new economic policy followed soon after.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain the rise of Dalit politics in the 1980s.", a: "1. Kanshi Ram formed BAMSEF in 1978, an organisation of backward and minority employees.\n2. He founded the Bahujan Samaj Party (BSP) in 1984 to mobilise Dalits and other marginalised groups.\n3. The BSP later formed governments in Uttar Pradesh, showing the growing political influence of Dalits.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the main developments relating to the Ayodhya dispute from 1986 to 1992.", a: "1. In February 1986 a Faizabad court ordered that the Babri Masjid premises be unlocked.\n2. From the late 1980s the issue became a major political matter; in 1990 the BJP's Rath Yatra from Somnath to Ayodhya mobilised support.\n3. On 6 December 1992 the mosque was demolished, which was followed by riots in many places.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the coalition era in Indian politics, 1989–2014, and why regional parties became important.", a: "1. From 1989 to 2014 no single party won a majority in the Lok Sabha, so governments were formed by alliances.\n2. Examples are the National Front (1989), the United Front (1996), the NDA (1998–2004) and the UPA (2004–14).\n3. Since national parties needed their support, regional parties became important partners in governing the country.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What was the Mandal issue? Explain its effects on Indian politics.", a: "1. The Mandal Commission (Second Backward Classes Commission) was set up in 1978 under B.P. Mandal; in 1980 it recommended 27% reservation for OBCs in educational institutions and government jobs.\n2. In August 1990 the National Front government decided to implement it for central government jobs, which caused violent protests.\n3. The Supreme Court upheld the decision in the Indra Sawhney case (1992).\n4. It brought the OBCs' political claims to the centre of politics and increased the influence of OBC leaders and parties.\n5. It contributed to the end of the Congress system and to the later consensus among parties on accepting the claims of the backward castes.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain the four elements of the growing consensus among political parties since the 1990s.", a: "1. New economic policies: most parties agree on liberalisation, though they differ on details.\n2. Claims of backward castes: parties accept the political and social claims of the backward castes, including reservations in education and jobs.\n3. Role of state-level parties: the distinction between regional and national parties has weakened and state parties share power at the Centre.\n4. Pragmatism: considerations of practical benefit, rather than ideology, shape alliances.\n5. Alliances are formed without ideological agreement, so competition continues, but within this broad consensus.", marks: 5 },
      {
        q: "Case study: In 1989 the Congress lost the elections and a National Front government headed by V.P. Singh was formed with outside support from the BJP and the Left. It was followed by Chandra Shekhar's government, which depended on Congress support. After the elections of 1991, the Congress formed a minority government under P.V. Narasimha Rao that lasted till 1996. From 1991, a new economic policy of liberalisation was also introduced.\n(i) Who headed the government formed in 1989, and who supported it from outside?\n(ii) What was special about the Congress government of 1991–96?\n(iii) Why was this period called an era of coalitions?",
        a: "(i) V.P. Singh; the BJP and the Left supported it from outside.\n(ii) It was a minority government, i.e. the Congress did not have a majority on its own but ruled with support from other parties, and it began the new economic policy of liberalisation.\n(iii) Because no single party won a majority in the Lok Sabha from 1989 to 2014, so governments depended on alliances among parties.",
        marks: 4, type: "case"
      }
    ]
